pocket-motion-only: MASK_PUSH is this repository's op (0013) and only the software rasteriser reads it — no upstream backend is taught it, and a stream carrying it stops there. The POLY half of the change is a unit change inside the row walk the two ops share, and leaves every POLY byte-identical.

0024 — an `overflow-mask` outline at sub-pixel precision.

The defect. `mask_outline` rounded every vertex of a window's outline to a
whole pixel before MASK_PUSH carried it, which moves each edge by up to
half a pixel on each axis. Along a long edge that is a band of pixels the
mask covers and the node does not — at full coverage wherever the rounded
edge sits outside the true one. And a square-cornered window at 0 degrees
emitted no mask wherever it stood: the scissor is its AABB rounded OUTWARD,
so at a fractional position, where per-tick translate keyframes leave every
moving window, a column of content outside the node.

The measurement is `a_mask_turned_about_its_corner_leaves_no_ink_outside_its_outline`:
a 120 px window turned about its bottom-left corner from -90 to +90 degrees
in quarter degrees, at four sub-pixel positions, holding a full-frame white
box. A pixel may be inked only when at least one of its 16 samples lies
inside the window's corners (to a sixteenth of a pixel). Before: 21,193
pixels inked with every sample outside, at 659 of 721 angles. After: none,
and every pixel whose samples are all inside is full.

The film it was found in (`mg-bauhaus`, frame 195, the 1 px sliver along a
rolling tile's edge): the sliver is the film's own declared geometry —
`MISREG.yellow = [1, 1]` in its `poster.ts` puts the yellow plate one pixel
into the next grid cell, and that cell carries the pixel when it rolls away.
With the outline rounded, the band crossed the window's edge; unrounded, it
lies wholly inside the window, where the film declares it. A content edge
drawn as POLY or TEX_TRI is still rounded to whole pixels — those are
upstream's op formats, and this patch does not change them.

What changes, by file:

- `contracts/spec/spec.ts`, `gen-rust.ts`, `engine/core/src/spec.rs`
  (regenerated): MASK_PUSH is 2 + 2N words, N `(x, y)` f32 pairs in logical
  pixels.
- `engine/core/src/draw.rs`: `mask_outline` clamps to the viewport and does
  not round; each corner quarter's two ends are taken on the axes exactly
  (Taylor `sinf`/`cosf` are a few ulp off at multiples of pi/2, and at
  `rounded-full` one corner's last vertex and the next one's first are one
  point only an exact pair deduplicates — a circle stays 48 vertices); the
  AABB shortcut needs all four AABB coordinates on whole pixels.
- `engine/core/src/raster.rs`: `PolyScan` works in sixteenths of a physical
  pixel (`POLY_SUB`), samples at +-2 and +-6 of them from a pixel centre.
  POLY's whole-pixel vertices enter multiplied by 16, which scales every
  edge function, row bound and step by the same positive constant and
  changes no sample's sign: POLY is byte-identical by construction.
  MASK_PUSH's f32 vertices enter rounded to the nearest sixteenth.
- `engine/core/src/damage.rs`, `lib.rs`, `compositor.rs`: the three
  draw-list walkers step 2 + 2N; damage bounds a mask by its f32 vertices,
  floor to ceil.
- `engine/core/src/tests.rs`, `damage.rs` tests: the sweep above;
  `mask_coverage_is_poly_coverage_for_the_same_vertices` replaces
  `mask_of_a_rotated_window_equals_poly_of_the_same_box` — a window's outline
  is no longer the rotated box's rounded POLY, so the claim the old test made
  is now made at the rasteriser: POLY, and a full-screen RECT under a
  MASK_PUSH of that POLY's own vertices, are the same bytes over the same 54
  cases (red with the level builder made binary, as before).

Ledgers, against the same tree without this patch: `compositions/masked`
moves — all 90 frames, and only pixels on its four windows' outlines (frame
45: 5,073 pixels, none by more than 113 of 255) — because every one of its
windows is rounded or turned. No other digest moves, POLY included, and
every `visibility` row is unchanged.

`git apply` skips everything above the first `diff --git`.

